// @trap memory access out of bounds
// THE BOUNDS POLICY, THROUGH THE `Buf` SURFACE (`buffer-design.md` §A4). VL adds
// no per-access check of its own and `std:buffer` adds none either: the ENGINE's
// bounds check is the memory-safety proof, and it is loud. A `Buf` describes an
// extent; it does not fence one.
//
// The width here is the interesting part. `store8` is a READ-MODIFY-WRITE over
// the containing 32-bit word rather than an `i32.store8`, and an emulation that
// widened the access could trap where the instruction would not. It cannot: a
// memory's size is a multiple of 4, so the word containing an in-bounds byte is
// itself wholly in bounds, and the word containing an out-of-bounds byte is not.
// The offset below is a megabyte past a memory that is one page long.
import { Buffer, store8 } from "std:buffer"

const b = Buffer(16)
b.store8(1000000, 1)
print("unreachable marker")
